\title{CFPS-Sim: A Reproducible Starter Stack for Cell-Free Protein Synthesis}
\author{CFPS-Sim project}
\date{\today}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
CFPS-Sim combines auditable formulation and reagent-cost accounting, versioned
DNA constructs, and a reduced GPU model of cell-free protein synthesis.
Sequence-derived resource demands support sfGFP simulations; PETase and
reteplase require separate folding and activity models. A source-aware stock
registry flags unresolved chemistry and conflicting prices, while a GPU
benchmark evaluates prediction on measured literature formulations with
complete-recipe and plate holdouts. Numerical challenges and synthetic
calibration test implementation consistency, not biological accuracy.
The project prepares an evidence-grounded experimental learning workflow;
kinetics remain illustrative, and improved performance in the Ginkgo
coopetition has not been demonstrated.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction: from autonomous laboratories to the CFPS coopetition}
Cell-free protein synthesis (CFPS) produces proteins using biological machinery
outside living cells. Its experimental accessibility makes it a useful setting
for learning how recipe composition, resource availability, and measurement
affect biomanufacturing. The Fall 2026 course \emph{How To Biomanufacture
(Almost) Anything} (HTBAA) combines lectures, cloud-laboratory work, and student
projects in a global teaching network\cite{htbaa}. Its CFPS platform presents a
``Cell-Free Synthesis Coopetition for Humans \& AI,'' linking Ginkgo Bioworks
with HTBAA\cite{competition}.

An important predecessor is the OpenAI--Ginkgo collaboration reported by Smith
et al. in the February 2026 preprint \emph{Using a GPT-5-driven autonomous lab
to optimize the cost and titer of cell-free protein synthesis}\cite{smith}.
The study connected GPT-5 experimental proposals to validated robotic workflows
and returned measurements for subsequent design rounds. It reported a 40\%
reduction in specific protein-production cost and a 27\% increase in sfGFP titer
relative to its benchmark. Those results concern the tested system and reaction
geometry; they do not establish performance for other proteins or this competition.

The current coopetition makes formulation design a shared experimental problem.
Its interface supplies reagent stocks, literature formulations, reference recipes,
and control measurements for sfGFP, PETase (TfCut2 cutinase), and reteplase.
Participants work within fixed reaction and dispensing constraints. The readouts
also differ: sfGFP uses calibrated fluorescence, whereas the enzyme targets use
activity assays. A recipe can therefore affect measured performance through both
expression and target-specific folding or activity.

The judging objective concerns production economics: minimize dollars per gram
of protein, or the target's applicable dollars per activity unit. Yield and
reaction cost must therefore be evaluated together. A higher-yield reaction can
have worse specific cost; a cheaper reaction can also have worse specific cost
if its output falls more than its expenditure. We use this minimization
direction throughout the project. The cached draft contains inconsistent wording
about ``maximizing'' specific cost; that wording is not used as our objective.
The current complete pricing and target-specific scoring conventions must be
confirmed against the competition's operative ledger\cite{competition_rules}.

CFPS-Sim is our computational contribution to this setting. We propose to use
recipe accounting to enforce feasibility and track costs, mechanistic simulations
to organize resource hypotheses, and Bayesian optimization to choose informative
experiments. The scientific question is whether these calculations improve
confirmed specific production cost over a data-only baseline under the same
experimental budget. The present executable scaffold establishes
the workflow; calibration and prospective experimental comparison remain future
milestones.

Figure~\ref{fig:intro-resources} summarizes the central modeling problem:
sequence fixes substrate demand, while available machinery and competing
resources determine its dynamics. The experimental observable adds a separate
layer. Mature GFP, total protein, and active enzyme are different outputs;
improving one does not establish improvement in the others. The current model
represents an aggregate expression capacity, rather than independently measured
T7 polymerase and ribosome pools. Magnesium binding, pH, oxygen transfer, and
target-specific folding are priorities for subsequent extensions.

