\section{Production economics: controlling cost and increasing output}
For a recipe $x$ with transfers $v_i$ in nL and stock prices $p_i$ in USD/mL,
the dispensed-reagent cost and specific production cost are
\[
C(x)=\sum_i p_i\frac{v_i(x)}{10^6},\qquad
S(x)=\frac{C(x)}{Y(x)V},\quad V=20\times10^{-6}\ \mathrm{L},
\]
where $Y$ is the applicable protein titer in g/L. Minimize $S$ in USD/g.
Fixed lysate, base buffer, and DNA belong in the numerator along with supplements
and any charged diluent. Fixed inputs do not disappear when comparing recipes:
they set a cost floor and change the yield/cost tradeoff. A mixed stock is charged
once for its transferred volume, not again for its constituent chemicals.
For an activity concentration $A$ in U/mL, use $C/(1000VA)$ in USD/U and retain
the assay definition. Do not pool scores from different targets or silently
interpret activity-derived apparent g/L as measured protein mass.

An intervention improves specific cost precisely when
\[
\frac{S_{\mathrm{new}}}{S_{\mathrm{old}}}
=\frac{C_{\mathrm{new}}/C_{\mathrm{old}}}{Y_{\mathrm{new}}/Y_{\mathrm{old}}}<1.
\]
For example, a 5\% cost increase with a 15\% yield increase improves USD/g by
about 8.7\%; a 20\% cost saving with a 10\% yield loss improves it by about
11.1\%. Maximizing yield independently of cost is therefore not the competition
objective. We report both quantities and their cost/yield Pareto front, while
using the specific-cost ratio to rank eligible competition outcomes. Any extra
minimum-yield requirement must be justified by operative rules or a separately
stated practical objective, rather than imposed as an undocumented score change.

The partial cached draft ledger lists lysate at 4 USD/mL and sfGFP DNA stock at
1.933 USD/mL\cite{competition_rules}. Their fixed transfers alone cost 0.023866
USD per reaction before base buffer and supplements. The listed ATP stock costs
1.539 USD/mL, compared with 27.937, 39.343, and 37.846 USD/mL for CTP, GTP,
and UTP stocks. These prices apply to the particular listed stocks; concentration
and counterion definitions still matter. They motivate testing nucleotide
allocation and salvage rather than assuming that all nucleotide additions have
the same economics. The present model regenerates ATP only, so it cannot yet
predict the economic benefit of NMP substitution or other salvage chemistry.

The new cost ledger charges every positive transfer. Missing prices remain
missing, and a subtotal cannot produce a complete score. The cached draft
example has an unresolved base-buffer price; the separate illustrative ledger
explicitly assumes 0.1 USD/mL solely for demonstration. The implemented scope is
dispensed reagent cost, excluding labor, plates, assays, purification, and
compute. These other expenses belong in an experimental campaign budget or a
separate full manufacturing-cost model unless the official score includes them.
The official price snapshot and score scope must be frozen for comparisons.

\begin{Verbatim}
uv run cfps-sim cost examples/recipe.json examples/prices.draft.json
uv run cfps-sim score examples/recipe.json examples/prices.illustrative.json \
  examples/yield.synthetic.json
uv run --extra bo cfps-sim bo-cost-demo
\end{Verbatim}
The first command intentionally exits with status 2 because its ledger is
incomplete. The other examples use declared assumptions and synthetic output;
they do not score actual competition observations.

<<economics, echo=False, results='tex'>>=
from copy import deepcopy
from cfps_sim.economics import cost_recipe, score_recipe, pareto_indices
import matplotlib
matplotlib.use("Agg")
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import numpy as np
prices = json.loads((root / "examples/prices.illustrative.json").read_text())
partial = cost_recipe(recipe, json.loads((root / "examples/prices.draft.json").read_text()))
economic_cases = []
for label, volume, titer in [("Reference", 300, 1.0), ("Higher output", 600, 1.3), ("Economical", 150, 0.9)]:
    candidate = deepcopy(recipe)
    candidate["volumes_nl"].pop("nuclease_free_water", None)
    for reagent in ["ctp", "gtp", "utp"]:
        candidate["volumes_nl"][reagent] = volume
        candidate["stocks_mM"][reagent] = 100
    observation = {"target": "sfgfp", "value": titer, "units": "g/L",
                   "readout": "assigned illustrative sfGFP mass-equivalent titer",
                   "data_kind": "synthetic", "provenance": {"source": "Assigned illustration; not a simulated or measured yield"}}
    economic_cases.append((label, score_recipe(candidate, prices, observation)))
print(r"\begin{center}\begin{tabular}{lrrr}Illustrative condition & USD/reaction & g/L & USD/g\\\hline")
for label, row in economic_cases:
    print(f"{label} & {row['total_cost_usd']:.5f} & {row['observation']['value']:.2f} & {row['specific_cost']:.1f}" + r"\\")
print(r"\end{tabular}\end{center}")
front = pareto_indices([row["total_cost_usd"] for _, row in economic_cases], [row["observation"]["value"] for _, row in economic_cases])
print("The cost/yield Pareto front contains " + ", ".join(economic_cases[i][0] for i in front) + "; the Economical illustration has the lowest USD/g.")
print("The partial draft ledger leaves these prices unresolved: " + ", ".join(partial["missing_prices"]).replace("_", r"\_") + ".")
fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7, 3.4))
titer_range = np.linspace(0.6, 1.45, 50)
for specific in [2000, 3000, 4000]:
    ax.plot(titer_range, specific*20e-6*titer_range, linestyle="--", color="0.7", linewidth=0.8)
    ax.text(titer_range[-1], specific*20e-6*titer_range[-1], f"{specific} USD/g", fontsize=8, ha="right", va="bottom")
for label, row in economic_cases:
    ax.scatter(row["observation"]["value"], row["total_cost_usd"], s=45)
    ax.annotate(label, (row["observation"]["value"], row["total_cost_usd"]),
                xytext=(-8, 8) if label == "Higher output" else (5, 6),
                ha="right" if label == "Higher output" else "left",
                textcoords="offset points", fontsize=9)
ax.set(xlabel="Assigned titer (g/L)", ylabel="Dispensed reagent cost (USD/reaction)", title="Illustrative cost/yield tradeoff at 20 microliters")
fig.tight_layout()
fig.savefig("figures/economics.pdf", bbox_inches="tight")
plt.close(fig)
