\subsection{Cost/yield experiment selection}
The original BO smoke test optimizes only a synthetic yield surface. The new
\texttt{bo-cost-demo} fits log titer and selects noisy Monte Carlo expected
improvement in $\log(YV/C)$, applying known cost to both training-baseline and
candidate posterior samples\cite{bo_objectives}. This maximization is equivalent
pointwise to minimizing USD/g. Expected improvement in log utility is an
acquisition choice, not minimization of expected USD/g or a claim of risk-optimal
manufacturing. Modeling positive titer before applying the deterministic price
ledger keeps price changes separate from biological prediction. This avoids
learning the known cost function or dividing a yield-only acquisition by cost.

The demonstration uses toy prices, a fixed cost floor, and synthetic observations;
it has no calibrated recipe mapping or experimental batch model. One selected
point is an informative next experiment, not a confirmed economic winner.
For actual data, retain failed or below-detection observations and model their
measurement process instead of taking the logarithm of zero, dropping failures,
or creating an arbitrarily favorable ratio. Zero measured product is currently
reported as unscorable by the accounting command. Near-zero or uncertain outputs
need detection limits, replicate analysis, and independent confirmation.
Conditional GP intervals in the demo do not establish biological coverage.

Evaluate recipe changes by their effect on both numerator and denominator.
Prioritize productive use of the fixed machinery, expensive NTP additions and
their possible salvage pathways, target-specific folding, and systematic
reduction of supplements that show diminishing returns. Extra enzymes and
chaperones should remain only when confirmed output gains justify their cost.
Report raw yield, total reaction cost, specific cost, and price/yield provenance
for every panel. Candidate uncertainty may guide exploration, while final
ranking must use confirmed target-specific economics under a common ledger.

\section{Reduced dynamical model}
Concentrations are in mM and time is in minutes. Let $D$ denote fixed template
concentration, $M$ active machinery, $R$ mRNA, $P$ immature protein, $F$ mature
GFP, and $A$ the pooled amino-acid concentration. Define saturation functions
$s_N(z)=z/(K_N+z)$ and $s_A(z)=z/(K_A+z)$. Effective transcription and
translation rates are
\begin{align*}
v_{\mathrm{tx}}&=k_{\mathrm{tx}}DM\prod_{z\in\{ATP,GTP,CTP,UTP\}}s_N(z),\\
v_{\mathrm{tl}}&=k_{\mathrm{tl}}RM\,s_N(ATP)s_N(GTP)s_A(A).
\end{align*}
The effective rate constants $k_{\mathrm{tx}}$ and $k_{\mathrm{tl}}$ have units
of inverse minutes. The shared machinery variable is dimensionless and couples
both processes; separate polymerase and ribosome pools are a planned extension.
\begin{align*}
\dot R&=v_{\mathrm{tx}}-k_R R,&\dot M&=-k_M M,\\
\dot P&=v_{\mathrm{tl}}-k_{\mathrm{mat}}P,&\dot F&=k_{\mathrm{mat}}P.
\end{align*}
For each nucleotide $i$, transcription consumes $n_i v_{\mathrm{tx}}$;
the default demonstration uses placeholder counts $n_i=180$, while construct
mode calculates $n_i$ from the explicitly bounded transcript. Translation consumes
$L v_{\mathrm{tl}}$ pooled amino acids, $2L v_{\mathrm{tl}}$ ATP equivalents,
and $2L v_{\mathrm{tl}}$ GTP, with placeholder protein length $L=240$.
Construct mode obtains $L$ and each amino-acid count $c_a$ from the CDS and uses
$\dot A_a=-c_a v_{\mathrm{tl}}$ for the twenty individual pools. It replaces
$s_A(A)$ with $\min_{a:c_a>0}s_A(A_a)$, an illustrative limitation rule rather
than a calibrated charging model. Each pool starts at an illustrative 2 mM.
The supplied sfGFP CDS also has length 240, but its individual composition and
transcript demands replace the placeholders. Sequence-aware SBML exports retain
these counts and initial concentrations, enabling replay from time zero.
ATP regeneration uses fuel $E$ and ADP:
\[
v_{\mathrm{reg}}=k_{\mathrm{reg}}
\frac{E}{K_E+E}\frac{ADP}{K_N+ADP}.
\]
Here $k_{\mathrm{reg}}$ has units mM per minute. Each regeneration event consumes
one fuel equivalent and converts ADP to ATP. GTP, CTP, and UTP are not regenerated.
The ATP cost of amino-acid charging is lumped into ADP equivalents; it does not
represent the actual AMP and pyrophosphate chemistry. Nucleotide incorporation
and degraded mRNA leave the explicit nucleotide pools. These choices limit
biochemical interpretation while exposing resource depletion.

<<trajectory, echo=False, results='hidden'>>=
